<article class="prose">
  <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
  <h1>Reimbursements</h1>
  <p class="lede">Mark a purchase you expect someone to pay back. The category still records the spend.</p>

  <h2>1. Mark the purchase</h2>
  <p>Open the purchase and open <strong>More Options</strong>. Under <strong>Reimbursement</strong>, turn on <strong>Reimbursable</strong>.</p>
  <p><strong>Who owes you</strong> is optional. Employers are listed first. ZxBudget does not fill this in for you.</p>
  <p>You can also mark it from the register. Open the row menu and choose <strong>Reimbursable</strong>.</p>
  <p>Marking it does not set money aside. Category available, overspending, and Ready to Assign stay on the normal rules. Cover the overspend the same way you cover any other purchase. When the repayment arrives, Apply puts that money back into the categories the purchase used. A card purchase still needs its payment category covered as usual.</p>

  <h2>2. See what is still owed</h2>
  <p>While something is open, Home shows <strong>Awaiting reimbursement</strong>. Open it for the list and the <strong>Still owed</strong> total. The row hides when nothing is owed.</p>
  <p>The register can filter to <strong>Reimbursable</strong>. A row can show who owes you.</p>
  <p><strong>Reports</strong> has <strong>Reimbursements</strong> once the budget has any reimbursable purchase. <strong>Still owed</strong> is what is open now. <strong>Spent</strong> and <strong>Received</strong> are the month you are looking at. <strong>Shortfall</strong> is a deposit that did not cover everything and is still waiting. <strong>Surplus</strong> is extra that already went to Ready to Assign. <strong>Forfeited</strong> is what you do not expect back. Spending reports still count the purchase.</p>

  <h2>3. Apply the repayment</h2>
  <p>Record the deposit as an inflow. In <strong>More Options</strong>, under <strong>Reimbursement</strong>, choose <strong>Apply to reimbursements…</strong></p>
  <p>The sheet is <strong>Apply reimbursement</strong>. A <strong>Previous shortfall</strong> is paid first. Purchases whose <strong>Who owes you</strong> matches the deposit payee start selected. You choose the others.</p>
  <p><strong>Apply</strong> saves the deposit as what those purchases paid. <strong>What this deposit paid</strong> lists them. A paid purchase shows <strong>Paid by</strong>.</p>
  <p>If the deposit is short, the rest is a <strong>Shortfall</strong>. Turn <strong>Reimbursable</strong> on if you still expect that remainder. Leave it off and the remainder is <strong>Forfeited</strong>. You can mark it later.</p>
  <p>If the deposit is more than the purchases it covered, the extra goes to Ready to Assign. The report counts that extra as <strong>Surplus</strong>. Still owed does not change because of it.</p>

  <h2>4. Mark what will not be repaid</h2>
  <p>On an open amount, <strong>Won’t be repaid</strong> sets how much you are done waiting for. You can set <strong>None</strong>, <strong>All</strong>, or an amount in between. <strong>All</strong> closes it as <strong>Forfeited</strong>, not as paid. <strong>None</strong> puts the remainder back to still owed.</p>
  <p>There is no second transaction. Use it from the purchase, from <strong>Awaiting reimbursement</strong>, or from the register <strong>Reimbursable</strong> menu.</p>
</article>
